    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Readings taken from the bar chart are accepted within one small square of the value in the scheme, and the unit kg must be given. (3) In calculations award the method mark even where the final figure is wrong, provided the working is shown. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (6) Accept the name of any feed ingredient or farm animal common in the candidate's own district.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) The **quantity of feed, made up of different ingredients, given to an animal in one day to meet its needs** (1 mark).\n- (ii) The animal **grows fast and stays healthy and produces more meat, milk or eggs**; feed is not wasted and disease is reduced (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'the daily feed allowance of an animal' in (i).*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- Maize - **carbohydrate (energy)** (1 mark).\n- Fish meal - **protein** (1 mark).\n- Oyster shell - **minerals, chiefly calcium** (1 mark).\n- Premix - **vitamins and trace minerals** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'energy food' for maize and 'body-building food' for fish meal.*\n\n**(c)** [4 marks]\n\n- **Weigh out each ingredient** in the correct proportion for 100 kg (1 mark).\n- On a **clean, dry floor spread the ingredient of the largest quantity (the maize meal) first** and the smaller ones on top (1 mark).\n- **Mix the premix and the salt with a little maize meal first** so that the small quantity spreads evenly through the heap (1 mark).\n- **Turn the heap over with a shovel several times until the mixture is uniform**, then bag it, label it with the date and store it in a cool dry place on pallets (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Marks are for the steps, not for the order alone; a candidate who mixes the premix directly into the whole heap loses the third mark.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **P** (1 mark).\n- (ii) W - **rumen** (1 mark).\n- (ii) Z - **abomasum** (true stomach) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Do not accept 'stomach' alone for Z.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) The feed is **stored and softened**, and is later **returned to the mouth to be chewed again as cud** (1 mark).\n- (i) **Micro-organisms (bacteria) break down the cellulose (fibre)** of the feed by fermentation (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Pig** (accept fowl, rabbit or dog) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Either of the two points in (i) may be given in any order.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) P can feed on **bulky, fibrous roughage (grass, hay, silage, crop residues)**, while Q needs feed that is **low in fibre** (1 mark).\n- (i) Q must be given a **concentrate rich in nutrients, in small regular feeds**, while P can be allowed to graze over a long period (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Elephant grass, guinea grass, hay, silage, maize stover, groundnut haulms or cassava peels** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Any named local roughage is accepted in (ii).*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- The pig has a **simple (one-chambered) stomach and no rumen with micro-organisms**, so it **cannot digest the cellulose** in the grass (1 mark).\n- Most of the grass therefore **passes out undigested** and the animal gets too little energy and protein, so it remains thin (1 mark).\n- He should feed a **concentrate ration of maize, bran and a protein source such as fish meal or soya bean meal** (with clean water), using greens only as a small part of the feed (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The first mark requires the idea of cellulose or fibre.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **2.4 kg** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Ration M** (1 mark).\n- (iii) **2.4 kg - 1.2 kg = 1.2 kg** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The unit kg is required in (i) and (iii).*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **The higher the protein content of the ration, the greater the average weight of the birds** (1 mark).\n- (ii) Ration M supplied **too little protein"
