    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In a formula, accept the figures written on the line (Na2O) or as subscripts, but do not award the mark where a symbol is wrongly written, for example NA or nA for Na. (3) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (6) Where a candidate names a material sold or used in his or her own community that the scheme does not list, award the mark if the chemistry is correct.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Element** - a substance that cannot be split into two or more simpler substances by an ordinary chemical change; it is made of only one kind of atom (1 mark).\n- **Compound** - a substance formed when two or more elements combine chemically in a fixed proportion by mass, and whose properties differ from those of the elements in it (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Award the mark for the idea, not for the exact wording. Do not award a mark where a candidate says a compound is 'two things mixed together'.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- Iron - **Fe** (1 mark).\n- Sodium - **Na** (1 mark).\n- Copper - **Cu** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The second letter must be a small letter. Do not award the mark for FE, NA or CU, nor for the Latin name alone.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- The first dish holds a **mixture**; the black solid is a **compound** (1 mark).\n- In the mixture the iron and the sulphur **keep their own properties**; in iron(II) sulphide the properties are **new and different** from those of iron and of sulphur (1 mark).\n- The mixture can be made in **any proportion** and the parts **separated by physical means** (a magnet); the compound has a **fixed proportion** by mass and can be broken down only by a chemical change (1 mark).\n- Any other correct difference, for example that **heat is given out** when the compound is formed but not when the two are merely stirred (1 mark, maximum 3).\n  - *Marking note: Maximum 3 marks. A difference must be written as a comparison; a statement about only one of the two dishes scores nothing.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Test:** pass a magnet, wrapped in a piece of paper or cling film, over the material in the dish (1 mark).\n- **Result:** the grey **iron filings are attracted** and are lifted away, leaving the yellow sulphur behind, showing that the two substances are only mixed and not chemically combined (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept adding carbon disulphide or warm water as alternative separation tests only where the expected result is correctly stated. The magnet test is the expected answer.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- A **binary compound is a compound made up of two different elements** chemically combined (1 mark).\n- Any correct household example: **water**, **table salt (common salt)**, **quicklime**, **magnesium oxide**, **hydrogen chloride** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Do not award the example mark for marble, baking soda or a fertiliser, because each of these contains more than two elements.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- Table salt - chemical name **sodium chloride** (1 mark).\n- Table salt - one use: **seasoning food**, **preserving fish or meat**, or **making oral rehydration solution** (1 mark).\n- Quicklime - chemical name **calcium oxide** (1 mark).\n- Quicklime - one use: **making mortar or whitewash for building**, **treating acidic soil on a farm**, or **treating water** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The use must be a real use of that material. Do not award a mark for 'used in the laboratory' without saying what it is used for.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Iron filings and powdered sulphur are mixed** (in the correct proportion by mass) and placed in a hard-glass test tube or on a tin lid (1 mark).\n- The mixture is **heated strongly** until the reaction starts (1 mark).\n- **Observation:** the mixture "
