    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In an energy chain the forms must be written in the correct order; a chain in the wrong order earns no mark. (3) In calculations, award the method mark for a correct statement or substitution even when the arithmetic that follows is wrong, and award the final mark only when the unit is given. (4) Accept 'movement energy' for kinetic energy and 'stored energy of position' for gravitational potential energy. (5) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **The change of energy from one form into another** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Energy can neither be created nor destroyed, but only changed from one form to another** (1 mark).\n- (iii) **The joule (J)** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (ii) the words 'created' and 'destroyed' (or an equivalent statement that the total energy stays the same) are required.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Light (solar) energy to electrical energy** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Light energy to chemical energy** (1 mark).\n- (iii) **Potential energy to kinetic energy** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Both the starting form and the final form are needed in each answer.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Chemical → electrical** (1 mark).\n- **→ light energy (and some heat)** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A chain written in the wrong order earns no mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Conversion:** a change of energy from one form to another, with a correct example (for example electrical energy to kinetic energy in a fan) (1 mark).\n- **Conservation:** using energy carefully so that none is wasted, with a correct example (for example switching off lamps in an empty room) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Each mark needs both the meaning and the example.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- A - **the water stored in the reservoir (lake)** (1 mark).\n- B - **the dam (dam wall)** (1 mark).\n- C - **the pipe that carries the water down (penstock)** (1 mark).\n- E - **the generator** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'alternator' for E and 'water pipe' for C.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **Potential energy (stored energy of position)** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Potential energy changing into kinetic energy** (1 mark).\n- (iii) **Kinetic energy of the water changed into mechanical (turning) energy of the wheel** (1 mark).\n- (iv) **Mechanical energy changed into electrical energy** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'movement energy' for kinetic energy throughout.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **No energy is created or destroyed; it is only changed in form** (1 mark).\n- The energy given up by the falling water **equals the electrical energy sent out plus the energy wasted as heat, sound and the motion of the water leaving** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The second mark requires the idea that the wasted energy must be included in the account.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **The water is free and renewable, so the running cost is low**, while diesel must be bought and its price keeps rising (1 mark).\n- **It gives off no smoke or carbon dioxide and little noise**, so it does not pollute the air (accept: the reservoir also serves for fishing, water supply and transport) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Two distinct reasons are required.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **The law of conservation of energy** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Q = 1 000 - (600 + 50)** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Q = 350 J** (unit required) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Award the method mark for the correct subtraction even if the arithmetic fails.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **The 600 J of kinetic energy** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **The sound energy (50 J) and the heat energy (350 J)** (1 mark).\n- (iii) **600/1 000 × 100 = 60%** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (ii) both wasted outputs are needed for the mark.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Oil or grease the bear"
