    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Readings taken from the graph are accepted within one small square of the value in the scheme, and the unit cm must be given. (3) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (6) Where a candidate names a crop, a dish or a seed bed used in his or her own district that the scheme does not list, award the mark if the farming is correct.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) The **stage at which the crop, or the part of it that is wanted, is fully developed and ready to be harvested** (1 mark).\n- (ii) The **husks and silk are dry and brown**, the leaves have turned yellow, and the **grains are hard** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'the grain is hard and dry' alone in (ii).*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Germination -> seedling -> vegetative growth -> flowering -> maturity** (2 marks for the whole order correct; 1 mark where only one pair is out of place).\n- (ii) The plant **produces leaves, stems and roots and grows larger**, making and storing food, but it does not yet flower (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: No mark in (i) where two or more pairs are out of order.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- Early maturing - **okro, cowpea, garden egg, lettuce or other leafy vegetable** (1 mark).\n- Medium maturing - **maize, rice, groundnut or tomato** (1 mark).\n- Late maturing - **cassava, yam, plantain, oil palm or cocoa** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept any locally grown crop placed in the right group; the grouping, not the particular crop, earns the mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- She should **count back the ten weeks from the date she needs the maize** and sow at that time - about the **first or second week of October** (1 mark).\n- She should also make sure the crop will have **enough water (rain or irrigation) during those ten weeks**, and may sow in batches so that some maize is ready even if the weather changes (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'early October' or 'about the beginning of October' for the first mark.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- A - **flat bed** (1 mark).\n- B - **ridges** (1 mark).\n- C - **mounds** (heaps) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'level bed' for A and 'heaps' for C.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- A (flat bed) - **easy and quick to prepare** and suitable for small seeds sown close together on well-drained land (1 mark).\n- B (ridges) - **give a deeper layer of loose soil and allow excess water to drain away**, and make weeding and earthing up easier (1 mark).\n- C (mounds) - **give the deepest loose soil for large tubers such as yam** and drain very well on heavy or wet land (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: One advantage only for each bed; mark the first given.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **C, the mounds** (accept B, the ridges, where the reason is correct) (1 mark).\n- (i) **Reason:** the crop is **raised above the wet soil, so excess water drains away and air can reach the roots** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Yam** (accept cassava or sweet potato) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The reason must mention drainage or air for the roots.*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- A bed of **deep, loose, well-drained soil lets the roots spread quickly** and take in water, air and nutrients, so the crop grows fast and **matures earlier** (1 mark).\n- A **hard, compacted or waterlogged bed keeps air from the roots**, growth is slowed and the crop **matures late** or gives a poor yield (1 mark).\n- A well-prepared bed gives **even germination and even moisture**, so the plants grow at the same rate and the whole crop is ready at about the same time (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Award the third mark for any other correct point, such as the effect of the manure worked into the bed.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **About 41 cm** (accept 40 to 42 cm) ("
