    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In calculations, award the method mark for a correct formula or substitution even when the arithmetic that follows is wrong, and award the final mark only when the unit is given. (3) Accept kWh written as kilowatt-hour or as units of electricity. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) In a description of a practical activity the marks are for the steps and the observation, not for neat English. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Using energy carefully so that it is not wasted**, that is, doing the same work with less energy (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Switch off the lamps, fan and television when they are not in use** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Iron all the clothes at one time (ironing in bulk)** or **fit energy-efficient lamps** or **use daylight during the day** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (ii) the two ways must be different from each other.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) The iron is **heated up once instead of four times**, so less energy is used in heating it (1 mark).\n- (ii) An LED **gives the same light for much less electrical energy**, while a filament bulb wastes most of its energy as heat (1 mark).\n- (iii) The lid **keeps the heat and steam in the pot**, so the food cooks faster and less fuel is used (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A bare statement such as 'it saves energy' earns no mark; a reason is required.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **It reduces the electricity bill and the money spent on fuel** (accept: it makes the supply serve more people) (1 mark).\n- **It makes non-renewable fuels last longer and reduces pollution and the release of carbon dioxide** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Two distinct reasons are required; two ways of saying 'saves money' earn 1 mark only.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **No / I do not agree** (1 mark).\n- **Reason:** the food would warm up and spoil, and the motor must then work hard to cool the whole cabinet again, so little energy is saved; **it is better to keep the door shut, defrost it and keep it away from the cooker** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A candidate who agrees cannot gain the reason mark, because the reason must support a sound judgement.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- A - **the object (the lighted candle)** (1 mark).\n- B - **the pinhole / small opening** (1 mark).\n- C - **the light-proof box (body of the camera)** (1 mark).\n- E - **the image of the candle** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'aperture' for B and 'inverted image' for E.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) It is **real** (it is caught on a screen) (1 mark).\n- (i) It is **inverted (upside down)**; accept 'smaller than the object' or 'laterally inverted' (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Light travels in straight lines**, so rays from the top of the flame cross at the hole and reach the lower part of the screen, and rays from the foot reach the upper part (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'Clear' or 'bright' is not a property for the purpose of this question.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- Correct relation: **height of image / height of object = length of box / distance of the object** (1 mark).\n- Substitution: **height of image = 12 × 15 / 60** (1 mark).\n- **Height of image = 3 cm** (unit required) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Award the first two marks for correct method even if the arithmetic is wrong; the final mark needs the unit cm.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) The picture becomes **larger** (1 mark).\n- (ii) The picture becomes **brighter but blurred (not sharp)**, because overlapping patches of light reach the screen (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (i) accept 'bigger and less sharp'; the key idea is the increase in size.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) **P = IV** (power = current × voltage) (1 mark).\n- (ii) **I = P/V = 1 500/240 = 6.25 A** (unit required) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 6.3 A"
