    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In calculations, award the method mark for a correct formula or substitution even when the arithmetic that follows is wrong, and award the final mark only when the unit is given. (3) Accept Pa or N/m² for the unit of pressure. (4) In explanations about pressure the idea of the AREA of contact must appear; an answer that speaks only of force earns half the available marks at most. (5) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **The force acting at right angles on each unit area of a surface** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Pressure = force ÷ area** (accept P = F/A) (1 mark).\n- (iii) **The pascal (Pa)** (accept N/m²) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (i) 'force per unit area' is sufficient.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) The sharp edge has a **very small area**, so the same force gives a **large pressure** and cuts easily (1 mark).\n- (ii) Wide tyres **spread the weight over a large area**, so the pressure on the soil is small and the tractor does not sink (1 mark).\n- (iii) The wide foundation **spreads the load over a greater area of soil**, reducing the pressure on it (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Each mark requires the area of contact to be mentioned.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- Sucking **lowers the air pressure inside the straw** (1 mark).\n- The **greater atmospheric pressure on the surface of the drink pushes the drink up the straw** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: An answer that says the straw 'pulls' or 'sucks' the drink up, with no mention of pressure, earns no mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Activity clearly described**, for example pressing a drawing pin between the thumb and the finger, or pressing a nail and a coin into clay with the same force (1 mark).\n- **Observation given**, for example the point hurts (or sinks in) far more than the head, showing that the smaller area gives the greater pressure (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The observation mark is lost if the candidate only names an activity.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **Area = 0.6 × 0.4 = 0.24 m²** (1 mark).\n- (i) **Pressure = 480 ÷ 0.24 = 2 000 Pa** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Area = 0.4 × 0.2 = 0.08 m²** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Pressure = 480 ÷ 0.08 = 6 000 Pa** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The units m² and Pa are required; accept N/m².*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Position Q** (1 mark).\n- (ii) The **force (weight) is the same in both positions** (1 mark).\n- (ii) But in Q the **area of contact is smaller (0.08 m² against 0.24 m²)**, and pressure = force ÷ area, so the pressure is three times as great (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A candidate who says the block 'is heavier' in Q loses both explanation marks.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **The block will sink into the ground** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Lay it on its largest face (position P)** - the larger area lowers the pressure (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Stand it on a wide flat board or slab** - this spreads the weight over a greater area and lowers the pressure (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Each suggestion must be accompanied by the reason in terms of area.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **4 000 Pa** (1 mark).\n- Because **the area is unchanged and the force is doubled, so the pressure is doubled** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A correct long calculation (960 ÷ 0.24) also earns both marks.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Pressure = 600 ÷ 0.3** (1 mark).\n- (i) **= 2 000 Pa** (unit required) (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Force = 5 000 × 0.2 = 1 000 N** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Award the first mark for the correct substitution even if the division fails.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Pressure = 500 ÷ 0.05** (1 mark).\n- (i) **= 10 000 Pa** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **It doubles (to 20 000 Pa)**, because the area is halved while the weight is unchanged (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (ii) 'it in"
