    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In a diagram, award the marks for the correct stages and for each correct label with a label line that touches the part named. (3) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed, but 'nymph' and 'larva' must be clearly distinguishable. (6) Where a candidate describes a control measure used in his or her own district that the scheme does not list, award the mark if the science is correct.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- P - **head** (1 mark).\n- Q - **thorax** (1 mark).\n- R - **abdomen** (1 mark).\n- S - **wing** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'fore wing' for S.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- T (the **hind leg**) is used for **jumping** (and for pushing the insect into the air before flight) (1 mark).\n- The **antennae** are used for **feeling and smelling** - for finding food and sensing danger (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'for walking as well as jumping' for the hind leg.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- The body is divided into **three parts - head, thorax and abdomen** (1 mark).\n- It has **three pairs of legs (six legs) joined to the thorax** (1 mark).\n- It has **one pair of antennae on the head** and **wings on the thorax** (1 mark, maximum 2).\n  - *Marking note: Maximum 2 marks.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Biting and chewing** mouth parts (1 mark).\n- With them the insect **bites off and chews the leaves and young shoots**, leaving ragged holes, so that the plants cannot make enough food and the yield falls (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Do not accept 'piercing and sucking'.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- J - **eggs** (an egg pod in the soil) (1 mark).\n- K - **young nymph** (a nymph without wings) (1 mark).\n- L - **older nymph**, with wing buds (1 mark).\n- M - **adult** (the fully winged insect) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'nymph' for both K and L only where the candidate shows the difference, for example 'young nymph' and 'late nymph with wing buds'. Do not accept 'larva' or 'pupa'.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) **In a pod in moist soil**, below the surface (1 mark).\n- (ii) The soil and the pod **protect the eggs from drying up and from enemies** (and keep them moist until they hatch) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'so that the young have food and cover when they hatch'.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Moulting** (casting off the outer covering) (1 mark).\n- (ii) The **hard outer covering (cuticle) cannot stretch as the insect grows**, so it must be cast off and a larger one formed (1 mark).\n- (iii) **L is larger than K and has wing buds**, while K has no wings at all (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'ecdysis' in (i).*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Incomplete metamorphosis** (1 mark).\n- (ii) Because there is **no pupal stage**: the young stage (nymph) **looks like the adult** and only grows larger and develops wings at each moult (1 mark).\n- (iii) The **housefly**, the **mosquito** or the **butterfly** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (ii) either the absence of the pupa or the resemblance of the nymph to the adult earns the mark; both together are not required.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- Number of stages - **three** / **four** (1 mark for both).\n- Name of the young stage - **nymph** / **larva** (1 mark for both).\n- Resting (pupal) stage - **no** / **yes** (1 mark for both).\n- Example - **grasshopper** (or cockroach) / **housefly** (or mosquito or butterfly) (1 mark for both).\n  - *Marking note: Each row earns its mark only when both columns are correct.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- The **nymph looks like the adult**, while the **larva is quite unlike the adult** in shape (1 mark).\n- The nymph usually **feeds on the same food and lives in the same place as the adult**; the larva often feeds on different food and li"
