    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Accept the name of any waste material or manure used in the candidate's own district, for example cocoa pod husks, rice husk, groundnut shells or brewery waste. (3) Readings taken from the bar chart are accepted within one small square of the value in the scheme, and the unit kg must be given. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) In a practical description the marks are for the steps, not for neat English. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) **Material of plant or animal origin added to the soil to supply nutrients and improve it** (1 mark).\n- (ii) Manure is made from **plant and animal waste and adds humus, improving the structure and the water-holding of the soil**, while fertiliser is manufactured and supplies nutrients only (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'manure acts slowly, fertiliser acts quickly' in (ii).*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Farmyard manure** (1 mark).\n- **Compost** (1 mark).\n- **Green manure** (accept poultry manure, liquid manure or treated night soil) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Do not accept the names of inorganic fertilisers.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) Two plant wastes, for example **dry leaves and kitchen vegetable peelings** (accept weeds, maize stalks, cocoa pod husks, rice husk) (1 mark).\n- (i) Two animal wastes, for example **poultry droppings and cow dung** (accept pig dung, goat droppings, bone meal) (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Nitrogen** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Both examples are needed in each half of (i).*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- A sandy soil **loses water and nutrients quickly**, since the particles are large and the soil is poor in humus (1 mark).\n- The **humus in the manure holds water and nutrients in the topsoil** where the roots can reach them, and feeds the soil organisms (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A general answer such as 'manure is good for soil' earns 1 mark only.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- A - **coarse woody material: twigs, chopped maize stalks or stems** (1 mark).\n- B - **green plant waste: leaves, weeds, grass or kitchen peelings** (1 mark).\n- C - **animal waste: cow dung or poultry droppings** (1 mark).\n- E - **a cover of grass, palm fronds or old sacks** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept any sensible material for each layer, provided it is placed correctly.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) It **introduces the micro-organisms (bacteria and fungi) that cause the waste to decay** (1 mark).\n- (ii) So that the **different materials are well mixed and rot evenly** (1 mark).\n- (iii) It **keeps the heap moist and warm and protects it from heavy rain and the hot Sun** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'to keep flies away' as a second use in (iii).*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) To **test the condition inside the heap** from time to time (1 mark).\n- (ii) The heap is **working well: the organisms are active (it is warm) and there is enough moisture** (1 mark).\n- (iii) To **add water when the heap becomes dry**, since the organisms of decay need moisture (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (ii) accept 'decomposition is going on well'.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- The **micro-organisms respire as they break down the waste and release heat**, which is trapped inside the covered heap (1 mark).\n- The heat **destroys many weed seeds, insect eggs and disease-causing organisms** (accept: it speeds up the rotting) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'The Sun heats the heap' earns no mark.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- **Collect and sort the waste**, removing plastics, glass, metal and stones, and **chop large materials into small pieces** (1 mark).\n- **Choose a shady site away from classrooms and from any well or stream** and prepare the heap or pit (1 mark).\n- **Build the heap in layers** - coarse material, green plant waste, animal dung, a thin laye"
