    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Accept 'asteroid belt' and 'belt of small rocky bodies' as the same answer, and 'air' for 'atmosphere'. (3) The words meteoroid, meteor and meteorite must be used correctly; one written for another earns no mark. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Award the mark for a correct idea expressed in the candidate's own words. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) The **Sun together with all the bodies that move round it** (planets, satellites, asteroids, comets and meteoroids) (1 mark).\n- (ii) The **gravitational pull (gravity) of the Sun** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'gravity' alone in (ii).*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Asteroids** (1 mark).\n- **Comets** (1 mark).\n- **Meteoroids** (accept natural satellites or moons, or dwarf planets) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Do not accept 'stars' or the name of a planet.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) A **planet moves round the Sun**, while a **natural satellite moves round a planet** (1 mark).\n- (ii) Planet - **the Earth** (accept any of the eight planets) (1 mark).\n- (ii) Natural satellite - **the Moon** (accept a named moon of another planet) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A difference in size alone earns no mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- A planet must **move in its own orbit round the Sun**; the Moon **moves round the Earth** (1 mark).\n- The Moon goes round the Sun **only because it is carried along by the Earth**, so it is classed as a **natural satellite** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The idea that the Moon orbits a planet is essential.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- A - the **Sun** (1 mark).\n- C - the **asteroid belt** (a belt of asteroids, small rocky bodies) (1 mark).\n- D - a **comet** (1 mark).\n- E - the **orbit (path) of the comet** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'asteroid' for C where the candidate shows it is the belt.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **E is a long, narrow ellipse, while F is nearly circular** (1 mark).\n- (i) The **distance of D from the Sun changes greatly along E**, and one journey round E takes far longer than one journey round F (1 mark).\n- (ii) At the **part of the path nearest to the Sun** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'at perihelion' or 'when closest to the Sun' in (ii).*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) Between the orbits of **Mars and Jupiter** (1 mark).\n- (ii) They are **small, rocky (or metallic) bodies of irregular shape**, much smaller than a planet (1 mark).\n- (ii) They **move round the Sun in the same direction as the planets, in nearly circular orbits in about the same plane** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Either of the last two points earns the second mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- It would travel in towards the inner solar system; a **small body would burn up in the atmosphere and be seen as a meteor** (1 mark).\n- A **large one could strike the ground as a meteorite, forming a crater, destroying life around it and throwing dust into the air** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Either outcome, correctly explained, earns both marks only where the two cases are distinguished.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- P - the **nucleus** (1 mark).\n- Q - the **coma** (1 mark).\n- R - the **tail** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'head' for Q only where the candidate writes 'coma (head)'.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Position 3** (1 mark).\n- (ii) There the comet is **heated most strongly, so much more of its ice turns into gas and dust is released** (1 mark).\n- (ii) The gas and dust are **pushed far out by the radiation and the stream of particles coming from the Sun**, giving a long, bright tail (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'solar wind' for the stream of particles.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- The tail points **away from the Sun** (1 mark).\n- **Reason:** the gas and dust are **pushed outwards by the Sun'"
