    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Accept the local name of a product or an industry (for example alata samina for local black soap) provided the meaning is clear. (3) Where a scientific process is asked for, the named process must be a process - evaporation, fermentation, saponification, filtration - and not simply 'heating things'. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **A group of firms producing the same kind of goods or services** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Primary (extractive), secondary (manufacturing) and tertiary (service) industries** (2 marks for all three; 1 mark for any two).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'extractive, manufacturing and service' in (ii).*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **Cocoa buying shed / cocoa growing** (accept the sawmill's timber felling) (1 mark).\n- (ii) Any TWO of **bakery**, **palm oil mill**, **kente weaving**, **blacksmith's shop**, **sawmill** (1 mark each).\n- (iii) **The taxi station** (transport) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A firm may be placed only once; mark each part on its own.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- Order: **harvesting the fruits → boiling and pounding → separating (skimming) the oil from the water and fibre → packaging and marketing** (2 marks for the full correct order; 1 mark if one pair is out of place).\n- Scientific separation: **at the skimming stage, because oil is less dense than water and floats on it** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'pressing' for pounding and 'selling' for marketing.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) Raw material: **cassava roots** (1 mark); product: **gari** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **B** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Only the letter is needed in (ii).*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **The pressing squeezes out the liquid** (1 mark); **the three days allow fermentation, which sours the mash and helps to remove the harmful (cyanide-producing) substance** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **The heat drives off the remaining water as steam and cooks the starch** (1 mark); **the dry product does not support micro-organisms, so it keeps long** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'poisonous substance' for the cyanide-producing compound.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Fermentation** (1 mark).\n- (ii) Any TWO of: **a sour smell or taste develops**; **bubbles of gas escape**; **liquid drips from the bags**; **the mash becomes warm**; **the colour changes** (1 mark each).\n  - *Marking note: The observation must be something that can be seen, smelt, tasted or felt.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- Any TWO improvements each with a reason: **a motorised grater** - faster and gives an even mash (1 mark); **a screw or hydraulic press** - the same pressing force on every bag (1 mark); **a mechanical roaster or a fixed roasting time** - every batch roasted alike (1 mark); **better hygiene in the shed** - a safer product of steady quality (1 mark). Maximum 2 marks.\n  - *Marking note: No mark for an improvement without a reason.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- P - **the channel (and gate) that lets sea water into the site** (1 mark).\n- Q - **the reservoir pan holding the sea water** (1 mark).\n- R - **the shallow crystallising pan in which the salt forms** (1 mark).\n- S - **the heap of harvested salt** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'storage pan' for Q and 'salt pan' for R.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **Evaporation** (1 mark) and **crystallisation** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **A wide, shallow pan gives a large surface area for evaporation** (1 mark); **the sun supplies heat and the wind carries away the vapour, so evaporation is faster** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'Boiling' is wrong; no boiling takes place in the pans.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Dissolve the dirty salt in water and filter to remove the sand and rubbish** (1 mark).\n- **Evaporate the filtrate to obta"
