        "text": "A Basic 9 class at Winneba used the school microscope to look at cells taken from the leaf of a hibiscus plant and at a prepared slide of human blood. The teacher explained that the cells of a living thing are not all alike.\n\n**(a)** Explain the terms **specialised cell** and **tissue**.\n\n**(b)** (i) Write out the levels of organisation in a living thing, beginning with the simplest.  **[1 mark]**\n\n(ii) Give **one** example of a tissue and **one** example of an organ in a human being.  **[2 marks]**\n\n**(c)** Describe how specialised cells are formed in a dicotyledonous plant and in a human being.\n\n**(d)** State **two** advantages of cell specialisation to a living thing.\n\n---"
