    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In a diagram, award the marks for the correct shape and for each correct label with a straight label line that touches the part named. (3) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed, but 'xylem' and 'phloem' must be clearly distinguishable. (6) Where a candidate uses a local example of a plant or a food that the scheme does not list, award the mark if the biology is correct.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Specialised cell** - a cell whose shape and structure have changed so that it can carry out one particular job very well (1 mark).\n- **Tissue** - a group of similar cells that work together to carry out the same job (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'a cell adapted to perform a special function'. Do not award the tissue mark for 'a part of the body' alone.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Cell -> tissue -> organ -> organ system (-> organism)** (1 mark; the order must be correct).\n- (ii) Tissue: **muscle tissue**, **blood**, **nervous tissue** or **bone** (1 mark).\n- (ii) Organ: **heart**, **lung**, **stomach**, **kidney**, **eye** or **brain** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In (ii) do not accept 'skin cell' as a tissue or 'blood cell' as an organ.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Unspecialised cells divide** (by cell division) to produce new cells (1 mark).\n- The new cells then **differentiate**, that is they change their shape, size and contents so that each can carry out one particular job (1 mark).\n- In the plant this happens in the **meristems - the tips of roots and shoots and the cambium**; in the human being the dividing cells are the **stem cells**, and similar specialised cells then group together to form tissues (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The word differentiation, or a clear description of it, is needed for the second mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- Each kind of cell carries out its own job **more efficiently**, because its structure suits that job (1 mark).\n- The **work of the body is shared out (division of labour)**, so many different activities go on at the same time (1 mark).\n- It allows the organism to **grow larger and more complex** and to survive in its surroundings (1 mark, maximum 2).\n  - *Marking note: Maximum 2 marks.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- A - **epidermal cell** (1 mark).\n- B - **guard cells** (a pair of guard cells with a stoma between them) (1 mark).\n- C - **xylem vessel** (xylem) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: In B accept 'guard cell'. Do not award the mark for 'stoma' alone, since the stoma is the opening and not the cell.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- A - **protects the tissues beneath and reduces the loss of water** from the leaf (1 mark).\n- B - **opens and closes the stoma**, controlling the movement of gases into and out of the leaf and the loss of water vapour (1 mark).\n- C - **carries water and dissolved mineral salts from the root to the rest of the plant** (and helps to support the plant) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Only one function is required for each cell; mark the first one given.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- It is a **long, narrow, hollow tube**, so water flows through it easily (1 mark).\n- It has **no cross walls**, since the end walls have broken down, so the water column is not interrupted (1 mark).\n- It is made of **dead, empty cells with no cytoplasm or nucleus**, leaving the whole space for water (1 mark).\n- Its **walls are thickened with lignin**, which strengthens the tube and supports the plant (1 mark, maximum 3).\n  - *Marking note: Maximum 3 marks, one for each correct feature.*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Water passes into the guard cells by osmosis** and they become **turgid** (1 mark).\n- Because the **inner wall is thicker and less elastic than the outer wall**, the turgid cells **curve "
